  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nTODAY INTO FRIDAY:\n\nLight, spotty snow snow showers in central and northeast\nMinnesota from this past evening have largely ended as drier\nlow-level air begins to work into the area there. Meanwhile,\nlight lake-effect snow showers are ramping up for the South\nShore with Lake Superior surface temperatures of 8 to 9 degC \nand 850 mb temperatures around -8 to -10 degC. Model thermal\nprofiles show 200-300 J/kg of lake-induced instability. However,\nthe atmosphere will only be saturated in the surface to 750-800 \nmb layer throughout the duration of the lake-effect snow today\nand Friday, only partially reaching into the dendritic growth\nzone at times with much drier air above that layer. Therefore,\nwe are not looking at the most efficient lake-effect snow\nprocess, which should largely keep snow rates on the light to\noccasionally moderate level. \n\nAntecedent above-freezing ground temperatures and current \nabove-freezing air temperatures should dampen initial \naccumulations a bit, but we eventually see portions of the South\nShore drop below freezing early to mid morning. Once we reach \nlater this morning through afternoon, high temperatures should \nonce again bounce above freezing into the mid 30s for most \nlocations along the South Shore, limiting daytime accumulation \npotential. Temperatures falling back below freezing tonight into\nearly Friday should allow for light accumulating snow again\nbefore the daytime limiting process to accumulations resumes on\nFriday. Eventually, by late Friday, a slight warming in 850 mb\ntemperatures and drier air working in alongside north-\nnorthwesterly winds should end the lake-effect snowfall.\n\nOverall, additional snowfall amounts remain light, producing 1\"\nto locally 3\" in the higher terrain of Ashland and Iron\ncounties, with surrounding areas moreso in the dusting to half\ninch range in other parts of Ashland, Iron, and southeastern\nBayfield counties, as well as north-central Wisconsin.\n\nOutside of the South Shore lake-effect snow and clouds, expect \ndry and mostly cloudy conditions today and mostly sunny skies \nfor the remainder of the area on Friday, with high temperatures\nin the low to upper 30s both days.\n\nTHIS WEEKEND THROUGH EARLY NEXT WEEK:\n\nHigh pressure moves into the Great Lakes region for this weekend\ninto early next week along with upper-level ridging which will\nlead to drier conditions and a slow warming trend. High\ntemperatures in the mid 30s to low 40s on Saturday reach \nwidespread 40s to around 50F on Monday and mid 40s to low/mid\n50s on Tuesday. \n\nMID TO LATE NEXT WEEK:\n\nGlobal deterministic and ensemble models still show good \nagreement on mid- and upper-level troughing sliding into the \nFour Corners region by Wednesday, with a trough/closed low \nejecting northeast into the Upper Midwest sometime around \nThursday, which is slightly slower than the forecast from 12-24 \nhours ago. At the surface, this would also produce a Colorado \nLow and track that system in the into the Northern Plains/Upper \nMidwest Thursday-Friday timeframe, leading to increasing \nprecipitation chances. The majority of global ensembles keep \nrain as the primary precipitation type as above average \ntemperatures are forecast to stick around into the latter half \nof next week.",
